Two facts worth stating here because they bite immediately, not because this
repo owns them:

- Every ContainerHub PowerShell module declares `#requires -Version 7.0`, so the
  entry scripts do too — launch with `pwsh`, never `powershell`.
- Workflows resolve ContainerHub's composite actions at `@main`, so an upstream
  change a workflow depends on must be pushed **before** the consumer change.

## 3. Critical invariant: submodule pins

<!-- Kept here rather than linked away with the rest of section 2 for two
     reasons: it is the invariant that makes every command in section 4
     meaningful (they are only supported against the recorded pins), and the
     coupling list below is genuinely project-specific - nobody upstream can
     write it for you. -->

Builds are only supported against the **recorded submodule gitlinks** — the commits
CI builds green. `git submodule update --checkout --recursive` restores every pin. If
a drifted submodule is what you actually want, update the gitlink **and** fix the
fallout in the same change.

<!-- FILL IN: the couplings a pin bump breaks in THIS repo. Shape:

       - `third_party/<X>` pins its own <dependency> (`<var>` in <file>); this
         repo declares `<var>` first and wins, so it must stay >= the pinned one
         or configure fails with <the exact error you get>.

     If there are none yet, say so — an empty list that was checked is worth
     more than a missing section. -->

Drift is guarded by ContainerHub's shared suite. It asserts that every configured
submodule is checked out at all, sits at its recorded commit, and is pinned to a
commit a fresh clone could restore — a pin that is on no remote branch works only on
the machine that made it:

```yaml
- uses: ./third_party/ContainerHub/.github/actions/run-pester-suite
  with:
    path: third_party/ContainerHub/shared/windows/tests/Submodule.Pins.Tests.ps1
```

The suite takes the repo root as a parameter, falling back to
`CONTAINERHUB_PIN_CHECK_REPO_ROOT` and then `GITHUB_WORKSPACE`, and the checkout that
runs it needs `submodules: true` — without it the suite FAILS rather than passing on
an empty tree, which is the point. Run it after any pin bump. It does **not** check
the version couplings above; those are on you.

